`AI_Post_Scheduler::init()` was a single monolithic method that instantiated ~20 admin controllers, all schedulers, and all services on every WordPress request regardless of context — frontend page loads, AJAX calls, cron jobs, and admin views all received the full object graph.

## Dispatcher

`init()` now delegates to exactly one context-specific boot method:

```php
public function init(): void {
    $this->boot_common();          // always: text domain, container bindings, taxonomy

    if (wp_doing_cron()) {
        $this->boot_cron();
    } elseif (wp_doing_ajax()) {
        $this->boot_ajax();
    } elseif (is_admin()) {
        $this->boot_admin();
    } else {
        $this->boot_frontend();
    }
}
```

## Boot Methods

- **`boot_common()`** — text domain, container bindings, `aips_source_group` taxonomy registration
- **`boot_cron()`** — scheduler/generator instances + cron hook registrations, `AIPS_Research_Controller` (for `aips_scheduled_research`), notifications event handler, partial-generation reconciler
- **`boot_ajax()`** — resolves the single controller for `$_REQUEST['action']` via `AIPS_Ajax_Registry` and instantiates it directly; falls back to `register_lazy_ajax_hooks()` for unregistered actions
- **`boot_admin()`** — `AIPS_Admin_Menu`, `AIPS_Admin_Assets`, `AIPS_Settings`, `AIPS_Onboarding_Wizard`, `AIPS_Admin_Bar`, notifications, reconciler. AJAX-only controllers are intentionally omitted — they're resolved on demand by `boot_ajax()`
- **`boot_frontend()`** — `AIPS_Admin_Bar` only

## Performance Impact

| Context | Before | After |
|---------|--------|-------|
| Frontend page load | ~20 controllers + all schedulers | `AIPS_Admin_Bar` only |
| AJAX request | ~20 controllers | 1 controller |
| Cron execution | ~20 controllers + all schedulers | cron-relevant classes only |
| Admin page view | ~20 controllers | menu/assets/settings infrastructure only |

## Tests

- Added stubs for `wp_doing_cron()`, `wp_doing_ajax()`, `is_admin()`, `load_plugin_textdomain()`, `register_taxonomy()`, and `remove_action()` to the limited-mode test bootstrap, backed by mutable globals
- Added `test-context-boot.php` (14 tests) covering: boot method existence and private visibility, context dispatch correctness (scheduler hooks absent in frontend/admin, admin_menu absent in cron/frontend), AJAX registry resolution, and cron-over-admin priority ordering

The category combobox accepted typed input but had no keyboard navigation — users had to type the full category name to select it, with no arrow key or Tab support to pick from the filtered list.

## Changes

- **`CategorySelect` keyboard handling** — adds `handleKeyDown` covering `ArrowDown`/`ArrowUp` (move through options), `Tab`/`Enter` (confirm active item), and `Escape` (close). Pressing `ArrowDown` when the dropdown is closed opens it and focuses the first item.
- **Scroll tracking** — `listRef` + `scrollIntoView` keeps the highlighted item visible as the user navigates, matching existing `UrlAutocomplete` behavior.
- **Dual visual state** — `active` class marks the keyboard-focused item; `selected` class marks the currently-saved value so it's visually distinct when the dropdown reopens. Corresponding CSS rule added to `index.css`.

```jsx
function handleKeyDown(e) {
  if (e.key === 'ArrowDown') {
    e.preventDefault();
    setActiveIndex((i) => Math.min(i + 1, totalItems - 1));
  } else if (e.key === 'Tab' || e.key === 'Enter') {
    if (activeIndex >= 0) {
      e.preventDefault();
      select(activeIndex < filtered.length ? filtered[activeIndex] : input.trim());
    }
  } else if (e.key === 'Escape') {
    setOpen(false);
  }
}
```

`http.DefaultClient` has no timeout, meaning a hung UptimeRobot API connection blocks a reconciler goroutine indefinitely — under load, this can exhaust the entire worker pool.

## Changes

- **`client.go`**: Added `DefaultHTTPTimeout = 30s`, an `httpClient *http.Client` field to `Client`, and initialized it in `NewClient()` with the configured timeout. Timeout is overridable via `UPTIME_ROBOT_HTTP_TIMEOUT` env var (consistent with existing env var pattern).
- **`retry.go`**: Replaced `http.DefaultClient.Do()` with `c.httpClient.Do()`. Includes a nil-guard fallback to `&http.Client{Timeout: DefaultHTTPTimeout}` for backward compatibility with directly-constructed `Client{}` test structs.
- **`retry_test.go`**: Added `TestNewClient_HasHTTPClientWithTimeout` and `TestDoWithRetry_HTTPClientTimeout` to verify correct timeout configuration and that a hung server is timed out promptly.

```go
type Client struct {
    httpClient *http.Client
    // ...
}

func NewClient(apiKey string) Client {
    return Client{
        httpClient: &http.Client{Timeout: cfg.httpTimeout}, // default: 30s
        // ...
    }
}
```

Tailwind CSS v4 depends on `lightningcss`, which requires a platform-specific native binary (e.g., `lightningcss.darwin-x64.node`). This binary frequently fails to install correctly, producing `Cannot find module '../lightningcss.darwin-x64.node'` errors that persist across reinstalls.

Nuclear option: remove Tailwind entirely and replace with CSS Modules, which are built into Next.js and require zero native dependencies.

### Dependencies removed
- `tailwindcss` v4
- `@tailwindcss/postcss`
- `lightningcss`

### Styling migration
- **`globals.css`**: Replaced `@import "tailwindcss"` with a CSS reset + `:root` custom properties (same color tokens as the previous `@theme inline` block)
- **8 components** → CSS Modules (`.module.css` per component): Navigation, Hero, About, Sectors, WhyChoose, Projects, Contact, Footer
- **2 components** → inline styles only (CursorFollow, SmoothScroll — already mostly inline)
- **`layout.tsx`** → inline styles for the 3 utility classes it used
- Responsive breakpoints (`sm:`, `md:`, `lg:`) → `@media` queries
- Group hover (`group-hover:`) → `.card:hover .child` selectors
- Global utility classes (`section-padding`, `gradient-text`, `glass-card`, `hover-lift`) retained in `globals.css`

### Example — before/after

```tsx
// Before (Tailwind)
<nav className="mx-auto flex max-w-7xl items-center justify-between px-6 lg:px-10">

// After (CSS Module)
<nav className={styles.nav}>
```

```css
/* Navigation.module.css */
.nav {
  max-width: 80rem;
  margin: 0 auto;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: space-between;
  padding: 0 1.5rem;
}
@media (min-width: 1024px) {
  .nav { padding: 0 2.5rem; }
}
```

Visual design is preserved — this is a toolchain change, not a redesign.

With 9 nav links in a `flex-wrap: wrap` / `justify-content: space-between` header, items spill into 2–3 uneven rows at most viewport widths. Footer links in a single `<p>` with `·` separators wrap awkwardly on narrow screens.

## Header
- Changed `.header-inner` to `flex-direction: column` with `gap: 6px` — title always sits cleanly above the nav row
- Made `.site-nav` a single horizontally-scrollable row (`overflow-x: auto; flex-wrap: nowrap; white-space: nowrap`) with the scrollbar hidden

## Footer
- Replaced the `<p>…link · link · link…</p>` pattern with a `<div class="footer-links">` flex container
- `.footer-links` uses `flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: center; gap: 6px 4px` so links tile cleanly across any width

```css
/* Before */
.header-inner {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;
  align-items: center;
  flex-wrap: wrap;        /* 9 links → multiple tangled rows */
  gap: 12px;
  min-height: 60px;
}

/* After */
.header-inner {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column; /* title row, then scrollable nav row */
  align-items: flex-start;
  gap: 6px;
  padding: 10px 0;
}

.site-nav {
  flex-wrap: nowrap;
  overflow-x: auto;
  scrollbar-width: none;  /* hidden but functional */
}
```

The map was receiving Transitland-backed features but not rendering stop markers, so users saw no stops despite backend data being available. This PR restores the client-side mapping from unified feature payloads to the stop source used by MapLibre.

- **Map feature rendering bridge**
  - Added `renderMapFeatures(features)` in `GetThere/Map/map.js`.
  - Handles `MapFeatureDto` entries from `MapPage` and routes `Stop` features into the existing `renderStops` pipeline.

- **Stop payload normalization**
  - Extracts stop fields from `feature.data` with fallback to top-level coordinates.
  - Normalizes to `{ stopId, name, lat, lon, routeType }`.
  - Filters invalid entries before updating the stop source.

- **No contract changes**
  - Reuses current `MapPage -> CallJsAsync("renderMapFeatures", features)` flow.
  - Keeps existing stop layers, icon logic, and schedule interactions untouched.

```js
function renderMapFeatures(features) {
  const stops = (features || [])
    .filter(f => (f?.type || '').toLowerCase() === 'stop')
    .map(f => {
      const d = f?.data || {};
      return {
        stopId: d.stopId || d.onestopId || '',
        name: d.name || 'Stop',
        lat: typeof d.lat === 'number' ? d.lat : f?.lat,
        lon: typeof d.lon === 'number' ? d.lon : f?.lon,
        routeType: typeof d.routeType === 'number' ? d.routeType : 3
      };
    })
    .filter(s => !!s.stopId && Number.isFinite(s.lat) && Number.isFinite(s.lon));

  renderStops(stops);
}
```

